Maybe the useful distinction is: wanting points toward something; needing makes your okayness depend on getting it. Wanting more can be healthy ambition. The snag is when “I’d love to have that” quietly turns into “I can’t be settled, respected, safe, or myself until I have it.”

Try this when you notice a want:

1. Name it plainly: “I want more money / recognition / freedom.”
2. Name what you hope it gives you: “I want the money because I want room to choose and provide for my family.”
3. Separate the goal from the value: “That goal could serve freedom. It isn’t the only way I can practice freedom today.”
4. Choose one step, then release the verdict: Do something that moves you toward it, without treating today’s result as a measure of your worth.

A quick test: Can I want this strongly and still imagine being okay if it doesn’t happen? If not, don’t scold yourself for “needing” it. Get curious about what feels threatened underneath, then find one small way to care for that need now.

You might use this sentence: “I want this because it would add something good. I don’t need it to prove I’m enough. I can pursue it and still be here before I have it.”

The practice isn’t wanting less. It’s keeping your hands on the wheel while the want rides along.